<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pandita Ramabai</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1858">1858</a>–<a href="/years/1922">1922</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Pandita Ramabai Sarasvati, Ramabai Dongre</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pandita Ramabai <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saraswati">Sarasvati</a> was an Indian social reformer and Christian missionary, born Ramabai Dongre into a Marathi-speaking Chitpavan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> family. Taught <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> by her father, she was the first woman to receive the titles Pandita and Sarasvati from the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>, in 1878. Widowed after an inter-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> marriage considered improper at the time, she founded the Arya Mahila Samaj in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a> to promote women's education and oppose child marriage, and gave evidence before the Hunter Commission. She converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, raised funds in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and founded the Sharada Sadan for child widows in 1889 and the Mukti Mission at Kedgaon.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pandita-ramabai.jpg" alt="Pandita Ramabai" width="330" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ramabai Sarasvati, Pandita, 1858-1922 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pandita_Ramabai_Sarasvati_1858-1922_front-page-portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After 1857 the British avoided intervening in religion, even over the remarriage of Hindu child widows, though sati had been banned before the rebellion; Indian reformers took up the cause, from <b>Pandita Ramabai</b>, who championed the remarriage of Brahmin widows, to Gokhale’s Servants of India Society.</blockquote><a href="/societies/british-raj">Social Hierarchy of British India</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangalore">Mangaluru</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Kedgaon, Pune district, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=12.87,74.84&amp;year=1899" aria-label="Mangaluru on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6604 1684 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pandita Ramabai"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7054 1954h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7054 1954h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=12.87,74.84&amp;year=1899">What was here in 1899? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;In ninety-nine cases out of a hundred, the educated men of this country are opposed to Female Education and the proper position of woman.&#8221;</p><footer>Evidence to the Education Commission, 1882 (The High-Caste Hindu Woman, 1887) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/ramabai/woman/woman.html" rel="noopener">digital.library.upenn.edu</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;After many years of careful observation and thought, I have come to the conclusion that the chief needs of high-caste Hindu women are:– 1st, Self-Reliance; 2nd, Education; 3rd, Native Women Teachers.&#8221;</p><footer>The High-Caste Hindu Woman (1887) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/ramabai/woman/woman.html" rel="noopener">digital.library.upenn.edu</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…who have done their best to keep women in a state of complete dependence and ignorance, vehemently deny that this has anything to do with the present degradation of the Hindu nation.&#8221;</p><footer>The High-Caste Hindu Woman (1887) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/ramabai/woman/woman.html" rel="noopener">digital.library.upenn.edu</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
